00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:12.770
here on the Kelly Clarkson show.

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:15.904
Good times, crazy stories,
unexpected confessions,

00:00:15.904 --> 00:00:19.830
wine, and our next guests,
'cause I love them.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:22.850
You know them from their
hit podcast and web series,

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:24.900
I Mom So Hard. They have a new

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:27.563
children's book, Meanest of the Meanies,

00:00:27.563 --> 00:00:28.520
(Kelly laughs)

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:30.340
I love y'all. Out right now.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:33.803
Please welcome Kristin Hensley
and Jen Smedley, everybody!

00:00:33.803 --> 00:00:34.773
(audience applauds)

00:00:34.773 --> 00:00:36.740
- Hi! Hello.

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:38.030
- [Kelly] How are you doing, ladies?

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:40.140
- [Jen] We are great,
we're happy to be here.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:41.660
I'm trying to make sure Kristin stays

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:43.120
on this couch and away from-

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:44.853
- [Kristin] I just wanna
be so close to both of you.

00:00:44.853 --> 00:00:46.595
(Kelly laughs)

00:00:46.595 --> 00:00:47.920
And I keep getting further away.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:50.240
And I know that's 'cause
I'm a slight security risk,

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:52.361
but I'm really just, I'm
really far away from you guys.

00:00:52.361 --> 00:00:53.760
- [Kelly] We push you to the end.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.040
- You guys are gonna,
like, put me in the side.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.210
- Next time you're going
to be with the band.

00:00:58.210 --> 00:00:59.130
- That'd be awesome.

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:00.270
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:02.397
- So you've been, you've been
busy since I last saw you.

00:01:02.397 --> 00:01:04.140
So, tell us about your
new children's book.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.160
I love the title, Meanest of the Meanies.

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:07.955
So, is it about mean kids?

00:01:07.955 --> 00:01:08.788
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:08.788 --> 00:01:09.920
- Well, they can be mean,

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:11.520
they don't mean to be, but they're honest.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:13.960
That's not always great. But it's just

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:16.250
the idea that no matter
what you do as a mom,

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:18.680
your kid views it as something
you're doing that's mean

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:21.110
or uncool or you're stopping their fun.

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:22.250
And all you're trying to do is

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:24.230
build a decent human, you know?

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:25.400
And the only way they're gonna thank you

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:26.780
is someday when they have kids,

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:28.430
but that's a long time from now.

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:30.160
So, we figured we'd
write a children's book

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:32.490
and celebrate among our, our mom crowd.

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:33.323
- Yeah.

00:01:33.323 --> 00:01:34.550
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:37.550
So, every mom in our
house seats can obviously,

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:39.920
you know, relate to the
highs and lows, hilarity

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:43.400
and humiliation of trying
to raise our kids right.

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:45.280
That's why we'd love to invite everyone

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:47.633
in our audience to Wine to Mommy.

00:01:49.031 --> 00:01:51.781
♪ Wine to Mommy. ♪

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:58.260
This is a safe space to
laugh, cry, commiserate.

00:01:58.260 --> 00:01:59.260
So, are you ready for this?

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:00.510
You've never done this with us before.

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:03.250
- I've never done this, I'm a
little nervous, but I'm game.

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:04.770
- She just, every time
somebody reaches front,

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:06.098
she's like sure, yeah, let's do it, yeah.

00:02:06.098 --> 00:02:06.931
(laughter)

00:02:06.931 --> 00:02:09.340
- I know I'm like, are
we fake drinking or we-

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:13.570
- I have not, no. I've had
like, totally a third of this.

00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:16.800
So, let's get to the
sharing, starting with Raven.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.690
So Raven, first of all, congratulations.

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:21.650
Your little one is almost here, right?

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:25.650
- Almost here, thank you.
Hi! How are you guys doing?

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:27.942
- Oh my God, you are so
beautiful. You're so cute.

00:02:27.942 --> 00:02:29.870
- [Raven] Oh, thank you.

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:31.510
- So, what's going on with you?

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:34.400
- Okay Kelly. So, I'm 35 weeks pregnant.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:37.800
This is our first child. Me
and my husband are expecting.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.210
We're just so excited for
her to be here so soon.

00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:43.090
And I see her little sweet
face, so we're so excited.

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:44.970
But, since the beginning of this pregnancy

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:48.490
we have been getting advice
from everybody, all the time.

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:51.160
Every day. Some people
are saying that we need

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:53.150
to rock our baby because that's how you

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:55.500
console and bond your baby.
And then others saying,

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:58.350
no, never let it rock
them. Let them cry it out

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:01.500
so that they get comfortable,
kind of, consoling themselves.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.320
We've had people even
say something crazy like,

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:05.810
if we want her to walk
early then we have to have

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:07.840
her in shoes all the time.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:09.470
Or even if I'm having crazy heartburn

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:11.980
it's gotta be because my
baby has a ton of hair.

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:14.366
So, Kelly, we just want
to know will we constantly

00:03:14.366 --> 00:03:17.480
bombarded with all these
questions all the time,

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:20.020
even after our baby girl gets here?

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:22.110
[Kelly And Kristin] Oh yes, yes, yes.

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:23.230
- [Kelly] And that was so annoying, too.

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:25.500
And I was, people are trying
to be helpful, I get it.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:28.630
But I was like, look, I'm
not looking to be that mom.

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:30.790
That's like, she has a tutor
and she's gonna end up in

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:32.670
Harvard and she's gonna
like. I'm not like,

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:34.140
I'm not, I'm not that mom.

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:35.710
Like, it's not, she's
going to end up there if

00:03:35.710 --> 00:03:37.220
she wants to be there. It's like,

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:39.190
let your baby walk when
they're supposed to walk,

00:03:39.190 --> 00:03:41.100
let your baby sleep when
it- but do it how you want.

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:43.550
I will say, now that I've said that,

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:46.540
the one thing I did listen to was a book

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:49.420
called Twelve Hours by Twelve Weeks.

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:51.690
And it's the only thing
I listened to. Everybody,

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:53.930
I read books, I did things,
like, with my first child,

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:56.070
and I was so worried.
That really did help.

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:58.090
It was the one advice I will
say that changed my life,

00:03:58.090 --> 00:03:59.300
the sleep training thing.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:01.640
But, at the same time,
do whatever's natural

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:03.700
for you and feels good
for you and your man.

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:05.920
Like it's like, it's
whatever you want it to be.

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:08.560
It's, it's gonna be that
way. I think any way is fine.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:09.393
- [Jen] Yeah.

00:04:09.393 --> 00:04:10.270
- Yeah, I think you have to, I mean,

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:13.360
for me, you know, I felt
like it was really important

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:16.990
for my child to feel safe in
his own room, in his own bed.

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:18.740
And also, I was a newlywed,

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:20.640
so I didn't want him in our bed with us.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:22.963
Like, I felt like that
wasn't a good thing.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:25.590
And so it was great, he loves his bed.

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:27.380
He loves his room. But,
then when I'd want to like,

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:29.010
just take a nap with him, he was not

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:30.380
interested in coming into my bed.

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:33.410
So it's like, just find
your way, you know?

00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:35.070
I feel like I do know a lot

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:38.470
of my friends who let their
kids sleep in their bed,

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:40.240
then couldn't get them out of the bed.

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:41.420
- [Kelly] I say no to that.

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:43.290
- [Juliana] So, I just
wasn't interested in that.

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:46.700
'Cause I think, you know,
happy parents, happy baby.

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:47.533
- [Jen] Yes.

00:04:47.533 --> 00:04:49.830
- And I think that's more
important, personally.

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:52.990
- Yeah. It feels like
everybody always has advice,

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:54.650
and things to tell a pregnant woman.

00:04:54.650 --> 00:04:57.320
Like we don't have, we're
we're just open to it.

00:04:57.320 --> 00:04:59.760
I say just be polite and ignore everybody.

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:02.110
But I was at Trader Joe's,

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:05.380
and this guy was helping
me with my groceries.

00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:07.510
No, I didn't know his name,
so I looked at his price,

00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:09.870
at his name tag. But he
felt he could tell me

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:12.250
where I needed to put
olive oil right before

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:14.210
I delivered the baby.

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:15.064
- What?!

00:05:15.064 --> 00:05:15.897
(audience laughs)

00:05:15.897 --> 00:05:18.160
- Turns out, olive can go to in pasta

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:20.590
and various parts of your orifices.

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:21.660
- [Kelly] Anatomy.

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:23.960
- Of your orifices. And
I thought to myself,

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:27.360
have you any idea that,

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:28.860
also that sounded like an awesome idea.

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:30.120
I did listen to him a little bit.

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:31.730
But, at the end of the day, I was like,

00:05:31.730 --> 00:05:34.310
you're not my OBGYN. You're
just a guy at Trader Joe's,

00:05:34.310 --> 00:05:35.780
but you feel like you're
comfortable enough

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:38.410
to tell me this very graphic birth story.

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:41.260
- We always think that, like,
the most important piece

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:43.070
of advice that you
should be told by, like.

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:47.770
Other moms should clue you
in on, is the first poop.

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:48.603
- [Kristin] Oh yeah.

00:05:48.603 --> 00:05:49.810
- And we're not talking about the baby's,

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:52.123
we're talking about yours after delivery.

00:05:52.123 --> 00:05:52.956
(Kelly laughs)

00:05:52.956 --> 00:05:54.780
- [Kristin] No one tells
you that, but honest to God.

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:56.000
- [Juliana] Don't scare her.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:57.660
- [Kristin] You can't, we're gonna-

00:05:57.660 --> 00:05:58.520
- [Juliana] You're scaring her.

00:05:58.520 --> 00:05:59.730
- [Kristin] Prepare
yourself, why do you think

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:00.860
you learn how to breathe?

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:04.670
- It's for, I mean. My son
was 7.4 when he was born,

00:06:04.670 --> 00:06:06.880
and that first poop was like an 8.6.

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:08.219
- [Kristin] 100 percent.

00:06:08.219 --> 00:06:09.280
(Kelly laughs)

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:10.370
- People don't warn you.

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:13.430
It is a weird experience
for your body afterwards.

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:14.600
You're like what?

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:16.820
- My band, y'all. They
actually also invited

00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:19.770
their mommas today. This
is Jessie's momma, Jamie.

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:21.820
Hi, Jamie, how you doing?

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:22.910
- [Jamie] Good, how you doing?

00:06:22.910 --> 00:06:24.710
- I'm doing all right.
I've got wine, Jamie,

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:26.170
so it's a good day.

00:06:26.170 --> 00:06:30.450
I hear you send Jesse songs
that we should do on this show.

00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:33.456
- Oh, I'm, I'm a big help.
You know, I'm a helpful mama.

00:06:33.456 --> 00:06:35.530
(everyone laughs)

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:38.003
Yeah, I have. I've sent
her some that I thought.

00:06:38.003 --> 00:06:42.330
I mean, the thing is, Kelly,
you can sing anything,

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:44.180
any genre, it's just astounding.

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:46.880
And of course, this band can do anything

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:49.613
and in a very short period of time.

00:06:49.613 --> 00:06:50.510
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:53.120
- Yeah, so I'm trying
to send them some songs.

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:57.590
I am a big lyric fan,
I love a great lyric.

00:06:57.590 --> 00:07:00.519
So, I've been on them for a
while about something like,

00:07:00.519 --> 00:07:05.016
from my generation, Desperado.
You know, Don Henley.

00:07:05.016 --> 00:07:06.057
- Love.

00:07:06.057 --> 00:07:07.800
- [Jamie] (indistinct)

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:11.412
Yes. Also, Clint Black.
Clint Black did that. Yeah.

00:07:11.412 --> 00:07:13.460
- [Jamie] It's a great
song. And then another,

00:07:13.460 --> 00:07:15.460
I kept thinking through
this whole last year

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:18.810
and a half, where we've
all been through so much

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:22.210
and we've had to learn to
be there for each other

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:25.450
even when we couldn't actually
be there with each other.

00:07:25.450 --> 00:07:27.500
I kept thinking of that genius,

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:30.410
Paul Simon and Bridge Over Troubled Water.

00:07:30.410 --> 00:07:31.491
I mean, you would kill that song.

00:07:31.491 --> 00:07:33.053
- Have we not done that?

00:07:33.053 --> 00:07:35.280
I think you might have
brought that up, Jesse.

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:36.240
- 'Cause she texted me.

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:37.849
- [Kelly] Oh, okay, that's why. Okay.

00:07:37.849 --> 00:07:38.800
(Kelly laughs)

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:40.680
No, okay. I was like,
that's a great song, too.

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:41.940
I can't believe we haven't done that.

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:43.410
There are so many great ones.

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:45.999
Please keep sending the songs,
we love the song requests.

00:07:45.999 --> 00:07:47.100
(audience applauds)

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:51.470
- Great, I'm all in. Jessie
knows, she's got the list.

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:53.390
- All right, All right.
Those, both are great.

00:07:53.390 --> 00:07:55.150
Let's add 'em, Jason.

00:07:55.150 --> 00:07:57.210
All right, well thank you Jamie.

00:07:57.210 --> 00:08:01.220
Everybody, let's get back to
this Wine to Mommy session.

00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:03.210
We have Lester's mamma, Pat,

00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:04.800
wants to join in the discussion.

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:06.137
So, Pat is great to see you.

00:08:06.137 --> 00:08:07.887
So, what do you want to talk about?

00:08:08.761 --> 00:08:11.450
- Yes, it's good to see you too.

00:08:11.450 --> 00:08:14.910
Mine is more of a grandma question.

00:08:14.910 --> 00:08:18.520
I am a proud mom and a proud grandmother,

00:08:18.520 --> 00:08:23.520
and I have 11 grandchildren and
three of which are Lester's.

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:28.550
And so, my thing is
when the grandkids come,

00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:30.340
they know that there are no limits.

00:08:30.340 --> 00:08:33.070
Me and Papa will go all out of our way

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:35.290
to make sure they're having a good time.

00:08:35.290 --> 00:08:40.290
We go to the movies, we go
out to eat, we go shopping.

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:43.650
I'm like here, yeah, go
ahead and buy you something,

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:45.980
we'll take care of it. Don't worry.

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:49.630
And so, am I just doing
a little bit too much?

00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:52.520
Or should I pull back some?

00:08:52.520 --> 00:08:53.650
- Let me, let me answer this, Kelly.

00:08:53.650 --> 00:08:55.824
Yes, Mom, you're doing too much.

00:08:55.824 --> 00:08:57.420
(Kelly laughs)

00:08:57.420 --> 00:08:59.830
- Lester's like, yes
you're doing too much.

00:08:59.830 --> 00:09:02.300
- I say do more. And especially,

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:05.090
a good way to spoil kids
is to add to the 529.

00:09:05.090 --> 00:09:05.923
- [Kirstin] Yeah.

00:09:05.923 --> 00:09:06.756
- That's a really, just cash.

00:09:06.756 --> 00:09:07.960
- [Kirstin] Every time
you take them to dinner

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:10.065
just mash that in their college fund,

00:09:10.065 --> 00:09:10.898
(Jen laughs)

00:09:10.898 --> 00:09:12.520
and then it doesn't feel so bad.

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:15.210
But, like, grandparents are
supposed to spoil the grandkids.

00:09:15.210 --> 00:09:18.200
That's your way to get back
at us for when we were unruly.

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:20.766
You know, I think it's,
I think it's nature.

00:09:20.766 --> 00:09:21.600
(audience laughs)

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:23.260
- [Kelly] We're not
gonna be helpful, Lester.

00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:25.800
- My answer's not helpful
either, because I do.

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:26.800
'Cause here's the thing though.

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:29.500
I look at them, 'cause my mom
will do stuff like that too.

00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:31.730
And, you know, spoil
them. And she loves it,

00:09:31.730 --> 00:09:33.580
and she doesn't see
them as much, you know?

00:09:33.580 --> 00:09:36.120
So, it's like, okay.
So, but I let her do it.

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:40.000
And it's just like, I tell
my kids, this is not normal.

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:43.140
This is grandma time. This is not,

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:44.970
what's going to happen every day.

